All user-adjustable parameters related to the shower are stored in
Cards/shower_card.dat. On top of various physics settings, the user can
specify the analysis procedure to adopt (see "Output and analyses" below).

PYTHIA8
=======
Pythia 8.2 or later is required (8.3 recommended), set with pythia8_path in
the MG5_aMC configuration. MG5_aMC writes the Pythia8 command file itself
(madgraph/various/mcatnlo_shower.py) and compiles the driver
pythia8/mcatnlo_pythia8.cc with pythia8/Makefile. The executable is kept in
MCatNLO/build/PYTHIA8_<hash> and reused as long as the sources, the analysis
and the Pythia8 installation do not change. HepMC output requires Pythia8 to
be configured with HepMC3 or HepMC2 (or hepmc_path to be set); events are
written in the HepMC2 ascii format. Their weights follow event_norm in the
run card: with average (default) or bias they average to the cross section,
with sum they sum to it; every event also gives the cross section (pb).
LHAPDF sets require the LHAPDF6 interface
of Pythia8; pdfcode = 0 uses the internal Pythia8 PDF.

Consistency with the MC@NLO subtraction
---------------------------------------
The MC counterterms of the short-distance cross section are computed for a
specific Pythia8 configuration. The choices made at generation time are
recorded in the <MCatNLOMatching> block of the event file (shower, MC@NLO-
Delta, FxFx, QED matching, Pythia8MMaxGamma, Born coloured multiplicity,
resonance decays corrected at NLO, ...), and the shower follows them even if
the cards were changed afterwards. Event files from older versions are
showered with the current cards, with a warning.

The command file (MCatNLO/RUN_PYTHIA8_<n>/Pythia8.cmd) has three groups of
settings:
 - required by the matching: starting scales (pTmaxMatch, pTmaxFudge), the
   global-recoil scheme of the first FSR emission of S events, the dead-cone
   recoil weight, SpaceShower:dipoleRecoil and rapidityOrder, the
   MC@NLO-Delta, FxFx and QED-matching settings, masses, lepton beams. They
   are listed in mcatnlo_locked.dat and checked by the driver after Pythia8
   is initialised: a tune, a plugin or any later setting that changes them
   stops the run ("MG5_MCATNLO_CONTRACT" lines in the log);
 - defaults, which pythia8_options can change: couplings (alphaS, alphaEM),
   PDFs, underlying event, hadronisation, decays, stable particles, tunes;
 - run control set by MG5_aMC (event file, numbers of events).
Changing a required setting with pythia8_options is refused, unless
allow_unsafe_shower_options = T, in which case it is applied, reported as
UNSAFE in the log and the NLO accuracy of the result is not guaranteed.
Settings unknown to the installed Pythia8 version are refused; a required
setting missing in an old version (for example the per-dipole starting
scales needed by MC@NLO-Delta) stops the run.

Matrix-element corrections follow arXiv:2308.06389: SpaceShower:MEcorrections
and TimeShower:MEextended are off, so that the hard process has no MECs (they
would double count the NLO real emission), while TimeShower:MEcorrections
(time_shower_me_corrections) keeps MECs in resonance decays. If the decay of
a resonance written in the event file is part of the NLO calculation (for
example Z > q q~ in e+ e- > j j), its MECs would double count as well and
TimeShower:MEcorrections is switched off.

Fortran analyses (ANALYSE) read the /HEPEVT/ common block, filled directly
from the Pythia8 event record (beams first, then the event in Pythia8
order); NEVHEP counts the analysed events from 1. HepMC is not needed for
analyses. An analysis whose NMXHEP differs from include/HEPMC.INC must call
hepmc_set_max_number_entries(NMXHEP) in its pyabeg.

HERWIG7
=======
Select HERWIG7 in the run card and configure its installation with
herwig7_path. Legacy HERWIGPP and HERWIG++ run-card selections are read
as HERWIG7 automatically. Herwig7 analysis files are in HW7Analyzer;
the HwU examples use the hw7an_ filename prefix.

Output and analyses
===================
There are three main options.

1) StdHep/HepMC output (default).
StdHep/HepMC n-tuples are stored at the end of the run in the file
  Events/run_**/events**.hep.gz (StdHep)
or
  Events/run_**/events**.hepmc.gz (HepMC).
Herwig6 and Pythia6 will produce StdHep files, while Herwig7 and Pythia8 will
produce HepMC files. The StdHep library is compiled the first time a Herwig6
or Pythia6 shower needs it (log in MCatNLO/stdhep_compilation.log).  These samples can be analysed afterwards with external
tools, like for example MadAnalysis5. This default option is used when
the ANALYSE variable is empty.
WARNING: the final files produced in this way are extremely large.
Please make sure to have enough disk space.

2) TopDrawer analyses.
The user can build her/his own analyses for TopDrawer output on the basis
of many examples in the MCatNLO/**Analyzer folders. Plots are stored at the
end of the run in the file Events/run_*/plot*.top.
To use one such analysis files (to be put in MCatNLO/**Analyzer) the user has
in principle to assign non-default values to the EXTRALIBS, EXTRAPATHS,
INCLUDEPATHS, ANALYSE variables.  For example, to shower p p > h HERWIG6
events with the ready-to-use mcatnlo_hwan_pp_h.f analysis, a typical setup is
  EXTRALIBS = stdc++ fastjet
  EXTRAPATHS = <path-to-fastjet>/lib
  INCLUDEPATHS = <path-to-fastjet>/include 
  ANALYSE = mcatnlo_hwan_pp_h.o mcatnlo_hbook_gfortran8.o myfastjetfortran.o
If the user has Fastjet in her/his own PATH, it is not necessary to fill
the EXTRAPATHS and INCLUDEPATHS variables. The use of FastJet, and of file
myfastjetfortran.f is required only if in the analysis there is a call to function
fastjetppgenkt.
Results from the various runs can be combined by means of the dedicated scripts
in the Utilities folder.

3) HwU analyses.
Same as for item 2) for HwU output. HwU is an internal plotting package that
allows flexibility in the output and in the combination of plots. The user can
write an own analysis on the basis of the many examples in the MCatNLO/**Analyzer
folders.
Output files are stored in Events/run_*, and results from the various runs can
be combined by means of the python script in bin/internal/histograms.py.

With NSPLIT_JOBS > 1, the input is split without parsing or rewriting the event
records. The temporary split LHE files and their shower-directory links are
removed after successful output collection; the original events.lhe.gz is kept.
Failed runs retain their inputs for diagnosis.

Compilation and shower logs are stored in Events/<run-name>/, with the shower
name and invocation number in their filenames (for example,
mcatnlo_PYTHIA8_1.log and mcatnlo_run_PYTHIA8_1.log). Split jobs also have
individual logs named mcatnlo_run_PYTHIA8_1__<job>.log. The combined shower log
contains every job, and later shower invocations keep the earlier logs.

Experimental NLO QED+PS runs can be enabled for one invocation with
  launch aMC@NLO --allow_qed_ps
or, in a generated process directory,
  ./bin/generate_events aMC@NLO --allow_qed_ps
The default QED+PS restriction remains in place when this option is omitted.
This option bypasses the launch restriction only; the matrix-element and
MC subtraction checks still run.

4) User's own analyses.
The user has to assign non-default values to the EXTRALIBS, EXTRAPATHS,
INCLUDEPATHS, ANALYSE environmental variables.  Analysis files have to be put
in MCatNLO/**Analyzer.
